---
interface Props {
  title?: string;
  content?: string;
}

const {
  title = 'Why use an image compressor?',
  content = '',
} = Astro.props;
---

<section class="py-16 md:py-20 bg-canvas dark:bg-[#111] border-t border-hairline dark:border-[#1f1f1f]">
  <div class="container-page max-w-3xl">
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-white mb-5 tracking-tight" style="letter-spacing: -0.6px;">
      {title}
    </h2>
    <div class="prose-content text-body dark:text-[#a1a1a1] text-sm leading-relaxed space-y-4">
      <slot>
        <p>
          Images are the primary contributor to web page weight. The average webpage loads over 2 MB of images,
          and a single high-resolution photo from a modern smartphone can be 5–15 MB. Serving these uncompressed
          directly impacts load time, bandwidth costs, search rankings, and user experience.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong class="text-ink dark:text-white font-medium">ImgCompressor Pro</strong> uses your browser's
          Canvas API to re-encode images at your chosen quality level. For JPEG and WebP, this means a
          lossy re-encode at 60–92% quality depending on your preset. For PNG, the canvas re-render
          strips metadata and performs a lossless re-encode, typically achieving 20–40% size reduction.
        </p>
        <p>
          Unlike server-based tools, nothing is sent over the network. This makes ImgCompressor Pro faster
          (no upload latency), more private (your images stay on your device), and reliable even without
          an internet connection.
        </p>
        <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-ink dark:text-white mt-6">When should you compress images?</h3>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1">
          <li>Before uploading photos to your website or CMS</li>
          <li>Before sending images via email (most mail servers cap attachments at 10–25 MB)</li>
          <li>Before uploading to social media platforms that apply their own aggressive compression</li>
          <li>Before adding images to presentations or documents</li>
          <li>When storage space is limited on mobile devices</li>
        </ul>
        <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-ink dark:text-white mt-6">What's the difference between lossy and lossless compression?</h3>
        <p>
          <strong class="text-ink dark:text-white font-medium">Lossy compression</strong> (used for JPEG and WebP)
          permanently discards some image data to achieve smaller sizes. At high quality settings (90%+),
          the difference is invisible to the human eye. At low settings (50–60%), artifacts may appear
          as blocky regions or color banding.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong class="text-ink dark:text-white font-medium">Lossless compression</strong> (used for PNG)
          retains every pixel exactly but achieves smaller sizes by finding more efficient ways to encode
          the data. No quality is lost, but the savings are smaller than lossy methods.
        </p>
      </slot>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
